<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
=============================================================================
 CardDemo modernization: Maven module descriptor for the REPORTING AND
 STATEMENT bounded context, published as com.carddemo:reporting-service and
 rooted at the Java package com.carddemo.reporting.

 PURPOSE
 One Spring Boot service re-expressing four COBOL programs: app/cbl/CORPT00C.cbl
 (online report request screen and its ad hoc job submission),
 app/cbl/CBTRN03C.cbl (133 column transaction detail report writer),
 app/cbl/CBSTM03A.CBL (statement generator, writing both an 80 byte plain-text
 and a 100 character HTML format) and app/cbl/CBSTM03B.CBL (its called
 input-output subprogram). The supporting contracts are app/cpy/CVTRA07Y.cpy,
 app/cpy/COSTM01.CPY, app/jcl/CREASTMT.JCL and app/csd/CARDDEMO.CSD. Every path
 is REFERENCE-ONLY: the COBOL baseline is the behavioural oracle for functional
 parity and is never modified. The uppercase .CBL, .CPY and .JCL spellings are
 the on disk spellings, so a lowercase citation is a dead reference. The
 field-by-field derivations are held in
 docs/architecture/cobol-to-service-traceability.md.

 WHAT THIS MODULE OWNS
 Nothing in the database: no schema, no table, no index, no constraint and no
 grant. Alone among the nine aggregator modules it owns no relational objects.
 It reads read-only cross-schema views through a database role holding
 SELECT-only grants, both bootstrapped by data-migration/sql.

 Assumptions: XML forbids two consecutive hyphens anywhere inside a comment,
 which the house comment style at .gitignore L40 to L65 uses for both clause
 separators and rulers, so this file deviates from house style deliberately:
 rules are drawn with '=', mid-rules with '~', line ranges are written
 "L499 to L505", and prose takes a colon or the word "because" where it would
 naturally take a dash. A future reader who "restores" the house dash style
 makes this POM unparseable, and because Maven reads it first that breaks every
 build in the nine module aggregator rather than only this module.
=============================================================================
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <!--
  Assumptions: relativePath is left at its default of ../pom.xml, which resolves
  the aggregator at services/pom.xml on disk. The grandparent
  org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-parent 4.1.0 is reached through
  that aggregator and supplies Java 21, the UTF-8 source and reporting
  encodings that the Checkstyle Checker charset must agree with, and every
  managed dependency version, so nothing here restates any of them. An empty
  relativePath element was rejected because com.carddemo:carddemo-services is
  never published to a repository, so a repository lookup for it could only
  fail. The consequence of this choice is a hard layout contract: this module
  must remain a direct child directory of services/.
  -->
  <parent>
    <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
    <artifactId>carddemo-services</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>reporting-service</artifactId>
  <name>CardDemo Reporting Service</name>
  <description>
    Transaction reports and account statements for the CardDemo migration.
    Serves the 133 column transaction detail report and the plain text plus
    HTML statement pair, and starts on demand report executions. Reads
    read-only cross-schema views under a SELECT-only database role and owns no
    relational objects of its own.
  </description>

  <dependencies>

    <!--
    Refactoring Rationale: Rule T2 of the migration replaces the COBOL copybook
    include path with Java imports, so one former COPY statement becomes exactly one
    type import from the single package that owns that contract. Under COBOL every
    program that needed a layout re-included it, so a layout change had to be
    re-verified program by program; a shared kernel makes those contracts
    single-sourced. Money, codecs, the error model, the keyset page envelope, the JWT
    role converter, the timestamp formatter and the validation flags are consumed
    only from com.carddemo.common.* and are never re-declared in this module.

    This is the ONLY intra-project dependency. No module may depend on another
    service module, and that prohibition is an ArchUnit test rather than a
    convention, precisely because it would otherwise be tempting here: every view
    this module reads is derived from tables other services own. Agreement with those
    services is reached through the physical database views, never through compiled
    code.

    Assumptions: the parent aggregator does not manage the intra-project coordinate
    com.carddemo:common-lib, so the version is stated on this one dependency as the
    inherited project.version property, so the module version and the sibling version
    are the same expression and cannot drift, where a literal string could.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
      <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: JPA is present in a module that never writes, which looks
    contradictory until the mapping is named. Hibernate arrives with this
    starter and supplies the Immutable annotation that marks each read-only
    view projection, so an accidental write is rejected in the persistence
    layer instead of travelling to the database and failing there against the
    SELECT-only grant. The projections are views, not tables, so schema
    generation stays off and this module contributes no DDL.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: the report request carries a date range, held in the baseline
    as the PARM-START-DATE and PARM-END-DATE card image fields at
    app/cbl/CORPT00C.cbl L105 to L112. Those dates were edited by COBOL
    paragraph logic before the job was ever submitted, so the same rejection
    must happen before an execution starts here. Bean Validation supplies the
    declarative half and com.carddemo.common.validation.DateEditValidator
    supplies the calendar half; both are needed because neither expresses the
    other's rules.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: identity is a validated Cognito JWT, and the cognito:groups
    claim is converted to Spring Security authorities by
    com.carddemo.common.security.JwtRoleConverter. This replaces the
    CDEMO-USER-TYPE field of the CICS communication area at
    app/cpy/COCOM01Y.cpy L26, whose admin and user domain is fixed by the
    condition names at L27 and L28 of that copybook. The replacement is a
    security correction rather than a like for like port: the communication
    area at L19 to L44 was storage the terminal echoed back, so a client could
    in principle assert its own user type, whereas a signed group claim cannot
    be asserted by the client at all.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: two independent consumers probe the /actuator/health endpoint
    this starter publishes, and they fail in different places. The container
    HEALTHCHECK in this module's Dockerfile uses it to decide whether the task
    is serving, and the ALB target group uses it to decide whether to route to
    the task at all. Removing this dependency therefore breaks the container
    health check and the load balancer health check together, and the second
    failure looks like a networking fault rather than a missing dependency,
    which is why the coupling is recorded here rather than left to be
    rediscovered.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: this module connects to the same Aurora PostgreSQL cluster as
    the six owning services, but under a role holding SELECT-only grants, and
    it reads only. That is the deliberate asymmetry against those services: a
    driver is required, a migration tool is not. That absence is not an
    oversight and is argued in the consolidated absences block below.
    Runtime scope is used because no compiled class in this module names a
    driver type; only the connection URL, supplied at runtime, selects it.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
      <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: the contract this publishes is OpenAPI 3.1, authored at
    src/main/resources/openapi/reporting-api.yaml, and ui/src/api/reporting.ts
    is written against it. That makes it a cross-boundary contract consumed by
    a separately built and separately deployed artifact, not internal
    documentation, so a breaking edit to it breaks the browser client rather
    than merely dating a document.

    Refactoring Rationale: both files named above now exist, and this comment is
    left in the present tense for that reason rather than by habit. It was
    written before either of them, so for a period it asserted as fact two paths
    that resolved to nothing, and a reader auditing the claim would have
    concluded the dependency below served a document nobody consumed. Two gates
    now hold the claim true rather than merely restating it:
    src/test/java/com/carddemo/reporting/api/ReportingApiContractTest.java
    fails the build if the contract is absent, unparseable, or disagrees with
    the metadata bean about its identity or its security scheme, and
    ui/src/api/contracts.test.ts fails the SPA build if the browser client and
    the contract disagree about which operations exist.

    Trade-offs: this starter serves a document GENERATED from this module's
    request handlers, which is a different artifact from the committed contract
    and is deliberately kept so. It is the instrument for the one disagreement
    no gate in this build can decide, namely whether a handler serializes the
    property names, types and widths the contract declares, because that
    comparison has Java on one side and YAML on the other and nothing here
    reads both as schemas. The cost is a second description of one surface; the
    benefit is that the undecidable half is at least observable.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
      <artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: metrics carry the service, environment and version common tags
    applied centrally by com.carddemo.common.observability.MetricsConfig, so
    this module adds a registry and no tagging logic of its own. The baseline
    had no metrics tier at all, only job log SYSOUT, so nothing here is a port
    and there is no legacy naming to preserve. Runtime scope is used because
    the registry is discovered by autoconfiguration and no compiled class in
    this module names a Prometheus type.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
      <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Refactoring Rationale: the baseline submitted an ad hoc report job by writing 80
    byte JCL card images to an extrapartition transient data queue, defined at
    app/csd/CARDDEMO.CSD L499 to L505 with DDNAME(INREADER), ERROROPTION(IGNORE) on
    L501 and RECORDSIZE(80) on L502. Three things were wrong with it, which is why
    the transport changes rather than being reimplemented. ERROROPTION(IGNORE)
    silently swallows write failures, so a request could be accepted at the screen
    and never reach the job entry subsystem; app/cbl/CORPT00C.cbl even carries an
    'Unable to Write TDQ (JOBS)...' message at L531 that the IGNORE option makes
    unreachable for exactly the failure it names. The request was serialised as raw
    JCL text rather than as data, so sort field positions were compiled into
    presentation strings. And the queue is fire and forget, leaving no handle with
    which to observe, address or retry a run. states:StartExecution answers all
    three: typed input, an execution ARN that makes the run observable and
    addressable, and an exception instead of an ignored error.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
      <artifactId>sfn</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: this module writes three fixed width artifacts to object
    storage, and every one of those widths is a contract inherited from the
    baseline rather than a choice made here. app/jcl/CREASTMT.JCL declares
    STMTFILE with DCB LRECL=80 at L87 to L91 and HTMLFILE with DCB LRECL=100 at
    L92 to L96, which is the origin of statements being produced in BOTH a
    plain text and an HTML form; app/cbl/CBSTM03A.CBL agrees exactly, carrying
    FD-STMTFILE-REC PIC X(80) at L45 and FD-HTMLFILE-REC PIC X(100) at L47. The
    transaction detail report is the third artifact, at the 133 columns fixed by
    FD-REPTFILE-REC PIC X(133) at app/cbl/CBTRN03C.cbl L85. Object storage
    replaces the catalogued sequential datasets those DD statements allocated,
    so the bytes are preserved even though the container for them is not.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
      <artifactId>s3</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: every endpoint and identifier this service needs is resolved at
    runtime from Terraform outputs, because no service hard-codes an endpoint.
    Parameter Store supplies the non secret values, namely the datasource URL,
    the state machine ARN for the report execution, the output bucket name and
    the JWT issuer URI; Secrets Manager supplies the database credentials for
    the SELECT-only role and the customer managed key references. That split is
    what makes the project constraint of no committed secrets structurally true
    rather than merely observed: there is no configuration file in this module
    into which a credential could be pasted, so none of these values appears in
    application.yml, in a tfvars file or anywhere else under version control.

    Assumptions: both starters below carry a version this module does not
    declare, inherited from the spring-cloud-aws.version property in
    services/pom.xml. That property records the one thing a reader of this file
    would otherwise miss: the pinned 4.1.0 line is built against Spring Boot
    4.0.7 and this reactor forces 4.1.0, deliberately and with the evidence
    stated there. Trade-offs: this module depends on the configuration-import
    half of that integration for the datasource URL, the state machine ARN, the
    output bucket and the issuer URI, so a startup regression here would present
    as a service with no configuration rather than as a missing feature, which
    is why the import path is exercised against a real endpoint by
    AwsStarterRuntimeIT in account-service rather than asserted in prose.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-starter-parameter-store</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-starter-secrets-manager</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: a controller slice test has no token exchange available to it,
    yet authorization is part of the behaviour under test, since the report and
    statement endpoints are guarded by the cognito:groups authorities described
    at the security starter above. This artifact mints a JWT directly, so the
    slice exercises the same JwtRoleConverter path production uses rather than
    disabling security to reach the handler, which would leave the guard
    untested precisely where it matters.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.security</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-security-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Trade-offs: a real PostgreSQL container starts materially slower than an
    in-memory database, and that cost is accepted because an in-memory database
    cannot prove either property this module's correctness rests on. It cannot prove
    that the SELECT-only role genuinely CANNOT write, because a substitute engine
    does not implement the same grant model and would report success on precisely
    the write production must reject, turning the one test guarding the read-only
    contract into a test that passes for the wrong reason. And it cannot prove that
    the views return the baseline's own card number then processing date ordering,
    fixed by TRNX-KEY at app/cpy/COSTM01.CPY L21 to L23 and the sort fields compiled
    into app/cbl/CORPT00C.cbl. A slower test able to fail for the right reason is
    worth more than a fast one that cannot fail at all.

    Assumptions: these are the Testcontainers 2.x coordinates, whose line renamed
    every module to the testcontainers prefixed form, so the 1.x names 'postgresql'
    and 'junit-jupiter' are absent from the BOM the parent imports. Verified against
    that BOM rather than assumed. Using the 1.x names leaves both dependencies with
    no managed version, and the resulting error names a missing version rather than a
    wrong artifact id, so it reads as a parent misconfiguration, and that misdirection
    is why the rename is recorded here.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers-postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers-junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Purpose: contributes the service-connection support that binds a started
    container to the datasource properties of a test context, so the query
    bootstrap under src/test/java/com/carddemo/reporting/repository does not have
    to restate a JDBC URL, a user and a password that the container already knows.

    WHY : Alternatives Considered: registering those three values by hand through
    a dynamic property source, which needs no dependency at all. Rejected because
    the container's URL is only known after it starts, so the hand-written form
    has to read three accessors in a static initialiser and keep their spellings
    aligned with the property names the datasource binds, and a misspelling there
    fails as a connection refusal at first query rather than as a missing
    property, which points at the database instead of at the test. The annotation
    form derives all three from the container itself, so there is nothing to
    misspell. Every sibling module that runs a container-backed test declares this
    same artifact for the same reason.

    Assumptions: the version is managed by the Spring Boot parent this module
    inherits, so no version is declared here.
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-testcontainers</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: the layering and money prohibitions are enforced as executable
    tests rather than as conventions, so a violation fails the build instead of
    depending on a reviewer noticing it. The rules held in
    services/common-lib/src/test/java/com/carddemo/common/architecture/LayeringRulesTest.java
    are that the money path admits no float, no double and no JSON number; that
    no service may import another service's domain package; and that a domain
    package may not import AWS SDK or web types. The third rule is load bearing
    in this module specifically, because it holds both an AWS SDK client and web
    controllers, so its view projections are exactly where such an import would
    otherwise creep in. That test file's path is itself part of the contract and
    must not be relocated, because the build names it in two places: the
    architecture-rules Surefire execution in services/pom.xml selects it by name,
    and the shared kernel's jar plugin execution packages its directory.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
      <artifactId>archunit-junit5</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Refactoring Rationale: the engine above was declared alone, and the rules were
    described as inherited from the shared kernel. Maven resolution passes a
    module's MAIN classes to its consumers and never its test classes, so that
    rule class was evaluated against the shared kernel alone; nothing analysed
    com.carddemo.reporting, and the third rule this module was said to depend on
    most was the one least enforced, because this is the module that holds an AWS
    SDK client and web controllers side by side. The shared kernel now publishes
    the class as a test artifact narrowed to the architecture package, and the
    architecture-rules Surefire execution scans it into this module's test run so
    the rules see this module's own compiled classes.

    Assumptions: this delivers one class and duplicates none, ownership staying
    with the file named above. Version and scope are managed by the aggregator, so
    neither appears here.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
      <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>
      <type>test-jar</type>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    =============================================================================
    DELIBERATELY ABSENT. Every entry below is a decision rather than an omission,
    recorded so that a future reader does not helpfully add one back. The
    dependency set above is complete.
    =============================================================================

    org.flywaydb:flyway-core and org.flywaydb:flyway-database-postgresql
    Alternatives Considered: (a) own a schema and migrate it, as the other seven
    services do. Rejected because this module is assigned NO owned tables: it reads
    read-only cross-schema views under a SELECT-only role, bootstrapped by
    data-migration/sql/V0__schemas_and_roles.sql and then
    data-migration/sql/V1__reporting_views.sql, neither of which this module owns.
    A migration tool here would claim ownership of objects this module must not own,
    and two owners of one object is how a schema drifts. (b) an Aurora read replica
    so reporting reads never touch the writer. Rejected because a replica adds cost
    and replica-lag semantics for no parity benefit: the baseline read the very same
    clusters the online programs read, so a replica would introduce a staleness
    window the golden masters cannot express. A missing view at runtime is a
    data-migration defect to report, and a src/main/resources/db/migration directory
    in this module is an affirmative defect.

    org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-batch
    Refactoring Rationale: this module STARTS a Step Functions state machine
    execution and returns; it does not run the job inline. batch-service owns the
    Spring Batch JobRepository and the batch.batch_run step ledger, which together
    supply the restart semantics the JCL RESTART= parameter never actually provided.
    A second job repository here would be a second, competing restart mechanism over
    the same runs.

    io.awspring.cloud:spring-cloud-aws-starter-sqs
    Alternatives Considered: model the report request as a queued message, as the
    authorization context models its request and reply exchange. Rejected because
    the baseline submission at app/csd/CARDDEMO.CSD L499 to L505 was not a request
    and reply exchange at all: no reply queue, no correlation identifier, only a
    one way write. An execution ARN is the faithful analogue of a submitted job
    and a queue would invent a contract the baseline never had.

    org.projectlombok:lombok
    Alternatives Considered: generate accessors to keep the DTOs and view
    projections short. Rejected because generated accessors cannot carry the Javadoc
    the explainability rule requires and the inherited Checkstyle gate does not
    relax skipAnnotations, so generated members would be reported as undocumented
    with no source location at which to document them. Java 21 record types with
    explicit constructors give the same brevity.

    org.mapstruct:mapstruct and its annotation processor
    Alternatives Considered: generate the copybook to DTO mappers. Rejected for two
    independent reasons. Its most recent published release is a beta, which is not
    acceptable anywhere on the money path; and the mapping here is not mechanical,
    because it drops FILLER, masks the primary account number to its last four digits, never
    serialises a card verification value, and applies the edit masks of
    app/cpy/CVTRA07Y.cpy. Each transformation needs an inline justification at the
    mapping site, and a generated mapper has no source line to hold one.

    io.github.resilience4j:resilience4j-spring-boot3,
    org.springframework.retry:spring-retry, and any circuit breaker starter
    Alternatives Considered: add a resilience library for retry around the state
    machine call and the view reads. Rejected because Spring Framework 7, arriving
    inside the Spring Boot 4.1.0 parent, moved retry into the framework core; the
    decision, its enforcement and the two easily mistaken API facts are recorded in
    docs/adr/ADR-002-compute-platform.md. A circuit breaker is omitted separately:
    the only synchronous hops here are in-VPC behind an internal load balancer with
    bounded timeouts, so a breaker would add a failure mode without removing one.

    Redis, ElastiCache, Kafka and Kinesis clients
    Alternatives Considered: cache the report and statement results, or stream them
    to consumers. Both rejected because the baseline has neither tier, so either
    would be new observable behaviour rather than migrated behaviour.

    org.testcontainers:testcontainers-localstack
    Trade-offs: the state machine submission could be proven against an emulated AWS
    endpoint, and that accuracy is declined in favour of a Mockito verified unit test
    asserting the StartExecution request this module builds. What is worth proving is
    which input is sent and that a failure propagates, and neither needs a container.
    =============================================================================
    -->

  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>

      <!--
      Assumptions: the documentation gate is INHERITED and deliberately not
      restated. The parent aggregator binds maven-checkstyle-plugin to the validate
      phase with failOnViolation true and violationSeverity warning, so the gate
      fires on every local build. suppressionsLocation is NOT set here because
      suppressions are wired from inside config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml by a
      SuppressionFilter whose optional attribute is false, and setting the property
      at the plugin as well would create a second, competing source for that path.
      Nothing in this file skips, relaxes, redeclares or lowers the gate, and
      nothing may: a gate that passes without checking is the single failure mode
      the gate exists to prevent.
      -->

      <!--
      Assumptions: this plugin is declared here rather than inherited, verified
      against org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-parent 4.1.0 whose build
      element carries a pluginManagement block and no bare plugins block, so the
      plugin is MANAGED there and activated per module. The managed declaration
      already binds the repackage goal and pins the version, so no version,
      execution or configuration is restated. Omitting it would still produce a
      green build and a plain library jar, with the failure surfacing much later as
      a container that cannot start, which is why the reasoning sits here.

      Trade-offs: finalName is deliberately not set, so the Dockerfile matches
      target/*.jar rather than a pinned name. The repackage goal leaves the
      pre-packaged artifact beside the executable one with a .jar.original suffix,
      which the glob does not match, so the apparent ambiguity does not exist; a
      pinned name would have to be kept in step with the artifact coordinates in
      two files.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>

    </plugins>
  </build>

</project>
